emely  disappointing right now and just to make  sure that's the case we actually did the  same export within davinci resolve and  here we actually got faster performance  compared to final cut we finished in  around a minute and 40 seconds once  again across all three of this machines  so davinci resolve is also not updated  yet to support the doubled media engines  but the big point that i do want to  mention is that during these exports we  measured the gpu usage to see how much  of an overhead you have just in case you  want to throw on a ton of extra graphics  lens flares whatever else and  surprisingly the m1 max was only using  53  of the gpu so you still have a bunch of  gpu overhead even with the 24 core gpu  and going to the craziest 64 core it was  only using 35 and i'd actually expect a  bigger difference but it's using 35  compared to 53 so basically there's  really no point  right now at least to go for the ultras  if you're just doing basic h.264 exports  and now over the last couple of years we  actually got a new standard that was  introduced and that's hevc or h.265  and that is actually more efficient  because you get a much smaller file size  you get better quality as well and as  you'll see right now you actually get  faster export speeds so we tested it in  final cut and that time came down to a  minute and 17 seconds for both m1 ultras  and actually a minute 23 for the m1 max  so for some reason it's a little bit  slower and the reason for that is  because the 24 core is just ever so  slightly bottlenecking it so it's five  seconds slower because it doesn't have  enough gpu power due to only having the  24 cores compared to the 48 and 64. but  with that said we actually threw the  same export test into davinci resolve  and our times came down to around 49  seconds each yes only 49 seconds for  this five minute export keep in mind  it's graded with two luts and film grain  that is very impressive so davinci  resolve is currently being better than  final cut and we actually ended up  looking at the gpu usage and even the m1  max was using 79 of the gpu so it's  almost perfectly balanced it still has a  little bit of wiggle room 21  of gpu overhead if you want to add a  little bit more but if you add a lot  more you are going to be throttled  compared to these other machines where  the mid machine was at 65 gpu usage and  52 gpu usage on the 64 course so there  are some diminishing returns there and  you have a lot of extra headroom if you  want to throw in a lot of extra graphics  and things like that but once again i do  want to mention that due to those  results around 49 seconds each these m1  ultras are definitely being throttled by  the encoders so they're just hitting the  limit and the extra encoders are not  being used at all so let's pivot over to  something that is purely gpu based yes  this is 8k redraw so it cannot take  advantage of any encoders any decoders  it's purely based on the gpu and here we  actually saw a similar story in terms of  diminishing returns going from the 24  core m1 max to the 48 core m1 ultra only  37.5  faster so doubling the gpu cores only  37.5 percent so  very diminishing returns there but then  going from the 48 core to the 64 we  actually got 23 faster so only five  minutes and 25 compared to six minutes  and 40 and that right there could  actually be a big deal for you guys  doing 8k redraw or whatever else redraw  you're doing 23 faster for a thousand  dollars that could actually be  worth it for a lot of people especially  since this is actually only a five  minute redraw export imagine if you have  an hour long project like you're gonna  save a lot of time going from the 48 to  the 64. so this could actually be worth  it but then that wasn't enough for us we  wanted to do something crazy so we did a  torture test yes we exported canon raw  that's 8k raw to 4k hevc that is our  torture test because it basically maxes  out the cpu and the gpu at the same time  an